<article class="env-lemma" id="05K8">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05K8">Lemma <span data-tag="05K8">10.28.1</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring. For a principal ideal $J \subset R$, and for any ideal $I \subset J$ we have $I = J (I : J)$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Say $J = (a)$. Then $(I : J) = (I : a)$. Since $I \subset J$ we see that any $y \in I$ is of the form $y = xa$ for some $x \in (I : a)$. Hence $I \subset J (I : J)$. Conversely, if $x \in (I : a)$, then $xJ = (xa) \subset I$, which proves the other inclusion. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>